{
  "title": "Files with pathlib",
  "summary": "Read and write text files the modern way.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Path",
      "body": [
        "`pathlib.Path` is the modern way to handle files, a mix of C#'s `Path`, `File` and `FileInfo`:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "from pathlib import Path",
        "",
        "p = Path(\"data\") / \"notes.txt\"     # / joins path parts",
        "p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)",
        "p.write_text(\"line 1\\nline 2\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")",
        "text = p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")",
        "p.exists(), p.name, p.stem, p.suffix   # True 'notes.txt' 'notes' '.txt'",
        "```",
        "",
        "Paths here are relative to the folder the program runs in. In the dojo each run gets its own fresh temporary folder, so write freely.",
        "",
        "> **Tip:** always pass `encoding=\"utf-8\"`. The default depends on the operating system."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "from pathlib import Path",
        "p = Path(\"reports\") / \"2024\" / \"summary.final.csv\"",
        "print(p.name)",
        "print(p.stem, p.suffix)",
        "print(p.parent.name)",
        "print(p.with_suffix(\".txt\").name)"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "summary.final.csv",
        "summary.final .csv",
        "2024",
        "summary.final.txt"
      ],
      "explain": "`suffix` is only the last extension. `stem` is the name without it."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Line by line with open()",
      "body": [
        "For big files, or to append, use `open` in a `with` block. Iterating a file object gives one line at a time (newline included):",
        "",
        "```python",
        "with open(\"log.txt\", \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:   # \"a\" appends, \"w\" overwrites, \"r\" reads",
        "    f.write(\"started\\n\")",
        "    print(\"also works\", file=f)       # print can target a file",
        "",
        "with open(\"log.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:",
        "    for line in f:",
        "        print(line.rstrip(\"\\n\"))",
        "```",
        "",
        "`Path` has the same: `p.open(\"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\")`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "match",
      "prompt": "Match each mode to what it does.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "`\"r\"`",
          "read (the default)"
        ],
        [
          "`\"w\"`",
          "write, emptying the file first"
        ],
        [
          "`\"a\"`",
          "write at the end"
        ],
        [
          "`\"x\"`",
          "create; fail if it exists"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "from pathlib import Path",
        "p = Path(\"todo.txt\")",
        "p.write_text(\"milk\\neggs\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")",
        "with p.open(\"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:",
        "    print(\"bread\", file=f)",
        "lines = p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines()",
        "print(len(lines), lines[-1])",
        "p.write_text(\"reset\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")",
        "print(p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").strip())"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "3 bread",
        "reset"
      ],
      "explain": "Appending adds a third line. `write_text` replaces the whole file."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Finding files",
      "body": [
        "```python continues",
        "folder = Path(\"photos\")",
        "for f in sorted(folder.glob(\"*.jpg\")):      # this folder only",
        "    print(f.name, f.stat().st_size)",
        "for f in folder.rglob(\"*.txt\"):            # recursively",
        "    ...",
        "p.unlink(missing_ok=True)                  # delete a file",
        "```",
        "",
        "`glob` order is not guaranteed, so `sorted(...)` it when order matters (and in tests, always)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "blanks",
      "prompt": "Write two files and list them in order.",
      "code": [
        "from pathlib import Path",
        "out = Path(\"out\")",
        "out.__1__(exist_ok=True)",
        "for name in [\"b.txt\", \"a.txt\"]:",
        "    (out __2__ name).write_text(name, encoding=\"utf-8\")",
        "print([f.name for f in __3__(out.glob(\"*.txt\"))])"
      ],
      "blanks": [
        "mkdir",
        "/",
        "sorted"
      ],
      "bank": [
        "makedirs",
        "+",
        "list",
        "create"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Notes file",
      "prompt": [
        "Write a script that reads commands from input until `end`:",
        "",
        "- `add TEXT` appends `TEXT` as a line to `notes.txt` (creating it if needed)",
        "- `list` prints every line of the file numbered from 1 as `1: TEXT`, or `no notes` if the file is missing or empty",
        "- `clear` deletes the file (no error if it is missing)",
        "",
        "Use `pathlib`."
      ],
      "starter": "from pathlib import Path\n\nNOTES = Path(\"notes.txt\")\n",
      "solution": [
        "from pathlib import Path",
        "",
        "NOTES = Path(\"notes.txt\")",
        "while (line := input()) != \"end\":",
        "    cmd, _, text = line.partition(\" \")",
        "    if cmd == \"add\":",
        "        with NOTES.open(\"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:",
        "            f.write(text + \"\\n\")",
        "    elif cmd == \"list\":",
        "        lines = NOTES.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines() if NOTES.exists() else []",
        "        if not lines:",
        "            print(\"no notes\")",
        "        for i, note in enumerate(lines, start=1):",
        "            print(f\"{i}: {note}\")",
        "    elif cmd == \"clear\":",
        "        NOTES.unlink(missing_ok=True)"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "name": "add and list",
          "stdin": "list\nadd buy milk\nadd call mum\nlist\nend\n",
          "expect": [
            "no notes",
            "1: buy milk",
            "2: call mum"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "clear",
          "stdin": "add x\nclear\nlist\nclear\nadd y\nlist\nend\n",
          "expect": [
            "no notes",
            "1: y"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "require": [
        {
          "pattern": "\\bPath\\b",
          "message": "Uses pathlib.Path"
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`line.partition(\" \")` splits off the command and keeps the rest intact.",
        "Open with mode `\"a\"` to append.",
        "`unlink(missing_ok=True)` deletes without failing."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
